I have generated R code using the &quot;debug&quot;mode in Distance. The code is cr=<br>eated by the DSM engine. Currently Distance only allows you to select fro=<br>m a few distribution families when using the DSM engine. Unfortunately I =<br>have many segments in my data set with no sightings - resulting in a very=<br> zero-inflated data set.<br><br>I would like to try to fit the DSM using a Tweedie distribution - which w=<br>ould be more appropriate for fitting a data set which is zero-inflated. I=<br> am however having a difficult time with the R code for this.<br><br>Here is the code generated by Distance from the DSM with a &quot;quasipoisson&quot;=<br> family:<br><br>if(sum(.packages(all=3DT)=3D=3D&#39;dsm&#39;)=3D=3D0)=20<br>  install.packages(&#39;C:\Program Files\Distance 6\dsm.zip&#39;, repos=3DNULL=<br>)<br>library(dsm)<br>file.create(&#39;C:\DOCUME~1\NICOLE~1\LOCALS~1\Temp\dst65008\res.r&#39;)=20=<br><br>file.create(&#39;C:\DOCUME~1\NICOLE~1\LOCALS~1\Temp\dst65008\stat.r&#39;)<br>source(&#39;C:\Program Files\Distance 6\support.r&#39;)<br>sample.dat.44&lt;-read.table(file=3D&#39;C:\DOCUME~1\NICOLE~1\LOCALS~1\Temp\=<br>\dst65008\sample.dat.r&#39;, header=3DTRUE, sep=3D&#39;	&#39;, comment.char=3D&#39;&#39;)<br>obs.dat.44&lt;-read.table(file=3D&#39;C:\DOCUME~1\NICOLE~1\LOCALS~1\Temp\ds=<br>t65008\obs.dat.r&#39;, header=3DTRUE, sep=3D&#39;	&#39;, comment.char=3D&#39;&#39;)<br>dsm.44&lt;-dsm.fit(ddfobject=3Dddf.2,response=3D&#39;indiv.est&#39;,model.defn=3Dlis=<br>t(fn=3D&#39;gam&#39;, family=3D&#39;quasipoisson&#39;),wghts=3DNULL,off=3Dcovered,link=3D=<br>&#39;log&#39;,formula=3Dte(midlat,midlon),obsdata=3Dobs.dat.44,segdata=3Dsample.d=<br>at.44,convert.units=3D1)<br><br>#theoretically I should be able to change the family from quasipoisson to=<br> tweedie in the above line of code but this does not appear to work - eve=<br>n when assigning a p value. I am hoping to use a pvalue of 1.1 for the tw=<br>eedie. (Tweedie(1.1))<br>Note: I have also loaded the Tweedie package and all other required packa=<br>ges in R to use a Tweedie Distribution<br><br># below is the remaining code<br>sink(file=3D&#39;C:\DOCUME~1\NICOLE~1\LOCALS~1\Temp\dst65008\res.r&#39;,app=<br>end=3DT)<br>cat(&#39;	Response Surface/Summary	
&#39;)<br>summary(dsm.44$result)<br>bmp(filename=3D&#39;gamcheck44.%02d.bmp&#39;,width=3D480,height=3D340,pointsize=3D=<br>12)<br>gam.check(dsm.44$result)<br>cat(&#39;	Response Surface/Plot: gam-check	
&#39;)<br>dev.off()<br>bmp(filename=3D&#39;resid44.%02d.bmp&#39;,width=3D480,height=3D340,pointsize=3D12=<br>)<br>plot(dsm.44$result$fitted.values,residuals(dsm.44$result,type=3D&#39;deviance=<br>&#39;), xlab=3D&#39;Fitted&#39;, ylab=3D&#39;Deviance Residuals&#39;)<br>cat(&#39;	Response Surface/Plot: residual vs fitted	
&#39;)<br>dev.off()<br>bmp(filename=3D&#39;diagnose44.%02d.bmp&#39;,width=3D480,height=3D340,pointsize=3D=<br>12)<br>plot(dsm.44$result, pages=3D, scale=3D0)<br>dev.off()<br>cat(&#39;	Response Surface/Plot: Diagnostic-Plot	
&#39;)<br>sink()<br>sink(file=3D&#39;C:\DOCUME~1\NICOLE~1\LOCALS~1\Temp\dst65008\stat.r&#39;,ap=<br>pend=3DT)<br>dsm.summary &lt;- summary(dsm.44$result)<br>cat(&#39;5010&#39;, dsm.summary$sp.criterion, &#39;
&#39;)<br>cat(&#39;5020&#39;, dsm.summary$n, &#39;
&#39;)<br>cat(&#39;5030&#39;, dsm.summary$dev.expl, &#39;
&#39;)<br>cat(&#39;5040&#39;, dsm.summary$scale, &#39;
&#39;)<br>sink()<br>rm(dsm.summary)<br><br>If any of you are wizards at R or have attempted to do something similar =<br>with your data I would love to hear from you.=20<br><br>Any suggestions or code which works would be greatly appreciated!<br><br>Thank you<br><br>Nicole<br><br>--<br>distance-sampling list home page:<br>  <a href="http://www.jiscmail.ac.uk/lists/distance-sampling.html" target="_blank" rel="nofollow" data-saferedirecturl="https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.jiscmail.ac.uk/lists/distance-sampling.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792477731217000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2XW9dv5Zip7noMrh2xofqn">http://www.jiscmail.ac.uk/lists/distance-sampling.html</a><br>To unsubscribe, go to the home page and click &quot;Join or leave the list&quot;,<br>  or email <a href data-email-masked rel="nofollow">jisc...@jiscmail.ac.uk</a> with the following message body:<br>  leave distance-sampling<br>Before posting to the list, read the list guidelines at<br>  <a href="http://www.jiscmail.ac.uk/files/distance-sampling/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow" data-saferedirecturl="https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.jiscmail.ac.uk/files/distance-sampling/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792477731217000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1xjGyIjZddXfG5xWikTdRd">http://www.jiscmail.ac.uk/files/distance-sampling/</a><br>    distance-sampling.welcome<br>Any questions, email the list owner at<br>  <a href data-email-masked rel="nofollow">distance-sam...@jiscmail.ac.uk</a><br>--<br>
